WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:03.650
I would start this with a gag about how there’s
nothing worth saying about Street Fighter:

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:09.030
The Legend of Chun Li, but I’m gonna be
talking for, like, twenty minutes straight,

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.599
so here we go,

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:15.800
Street Fighter: the Legend of Chun Li, a massive
failure of intent.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:22.420
[Title Theme]

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:26.340
Street Fighter: the Legend of Chun Li is a
very odd film.

00:00:26.349 --> 00:00:31.300
It feels like an honest attempt at taking
the video game adaptation and elevating it

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:37.550
to the same level of respect as literary adaptations,
and does so by mimicking…

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:38.890
The Transformers.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.210
You said cars pick their drivers.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:43.250
Sometimes they pick a driver with a cheap-ass
father, out the car.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:47.440
Now, this isn’t to say that the literary
adaptation is inherently high class, there

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.520
are definitely greater and lesser adaptations
out there

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:55.210
On one hand you have adaptations like Annihilation
that, while not strictly faithful to the original

00:00:55.210 --> 00:01:00.329
text capture the spirit of the novel in a
way that takes advantage of the change in

00:01:00.329 --> 00:01:04.129
medium, and on the other hand you have stuff
like Earthsea.

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:06.020
Did you see those red marking on the side
of its head?

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.120
Yes, very attractive.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.260
I've read about a dragon once with markings
like those.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:10.620
Fascinating, you can tell me by the fire one
night.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.000
Wait a minute. Red markings?

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.540
Like the dragon that ravaged Tavnor?

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.980
That's the one, do you remember it's true
name?

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.560
The one to bind it?

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.040
The one to subdue it to your will so you can
ask it any three questions you desire?

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:20.960
Yes!

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.560
but that specific spread in quality, and the
sheer volume of adaptations that have been

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:32.290
made, means that a movie based on a book tends
to get a pretty fair shake critically.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:36.401
The presuppositions created by the phrase
“based on the best selling novel” are

00:01:36.401 --> 00:01:39.160
broadly neutral, trending toward positive.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:44.180
The same cannot be said for the phrase “based
on the hit video game.”

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:48.920
The pursuit of the “respectable” video
game movie has remained elusive, but boy are

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.790
studios still trying.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:54.460
Tomb Raider and Assassin’s Creed would be
the most recent failed attempts at securing

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.010
some prestige, but let’s roll back a decade.

00:01:58.010 --> 00:01:59.070
It’s 2009.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:04.380
The Playstation 3 and XBox 360 are hits, and
high definition gaming is no longer next gen,

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.750
it’s the standard.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.890
Blockbuster video games are investing more
and more in narrative.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:12.810
Genres that would have previously done little
more than pointed the player in a direction

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:17.410
and just said “yeah, uh, shoot the dudes
in red” are expected to have a campaign

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:22.330
laden with pathos, melodrama, full voice acting,
and mocapped facial animation.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:26.840
Simultaneously so-called nerd culture has
surged into the mainstream with the better

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:31.500
part of the decade being dominated by huge
geek franchises such as Harry Potter, the

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.690
Lord of the Rings, Star Wars, and Transformers
dominating the box office and, to a large

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.310
degree, the cultural zeitgeist itself.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:44.910
In 2009 Capcom tried to capitalize on this
apparent respectability by turning Street

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:48.840
Fighter into an arty crime drama.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.760
By copying The Transformers.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.440
It did not work.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.900
I love this job

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.260
While the dramatic qualities of Michael Bay’s
2007 Transformers have been the subject of

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:08.980
endless ridicule and criticism, the spectacle
still resonated with audiences, and those

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.800
profits definitely resonated with investors.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.640
From aspect ratio to colour palette to story
structure, everything about Legend of Chun Li

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.320
is the budget-friendly version.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:24.860
Same aspect ratio, but using spherical lenses
instead of expensive, troublesome anamorphic lenses.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:29.720
Same colour palette, but in broad, washed
tones instead of an expensive frame-by-frame

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:30.959
colour grade.

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:36.220
Same plot structure with a superfluous Troopcop
b-plot that only collides with the a-plot

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:37.230
at the very end.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:41.790
It’s actually kind of unsettling the degree
to which Legend of Chun Li is clearly cribbing

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:47.180
from Michael Bay’s notes, all part of an
attempt at coding itself as a “real movie”.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:50.100
It even aspires to a similar weight and intensity.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:54.920
Not just action, but capital D Drama, which
brings us to the other primary influence,

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.650
Ang Lee’s Crouching Tiger.

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:02.340
On some level this project started as Crouching
Tiger, they wanted Legend of Chun Li to be

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:08.040
a martial arts action movie that was, at its
core, a character driven drama, much like

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.170
Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:15.709
That's not a bad goal, all told, and a character
like Chun Li isn't a bad choice if that's

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:17.660
the story you want to tell.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:22.190
Within the Street Fighter universe Chun Li
is easily one of the more accessible characters.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:25.840
She's an attractive martial artist who works
for Interpol, she's out to avenge her father's

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.100
death, and... that's about it.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.770
Unlike a lot of the other characters from
the games you don't have to exposit a deep

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:37.930
mythology, explain magic powers, or introduce
dozens of other core characters in order to

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:39.750
tell her basic story.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:45.330
It is a straightforward origins and revenge
plot, which frees you up to really focus on,

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:47.080
well, character and story.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:52.140
A good cue to the dramatic intent of the film
is in the specifics of the editing.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:55.980
The movie starts with a glory shot of the
Golden Gate bridge which pans over to kid

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:01.090
Chun Li playing piano and her father watching
proudly while adult Chun Li narrates over

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:04.830
top ruminating on her childhood dream of being
a concert pianist.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:10.280
This moment lasts almost a minute and has
only two edits for a very quick insert.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:15.350
From there the film cuts into a much faster
paced sequence of aerial shots of Hong Kong,

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:19.010
and the music changes from relaxed piano to
tense electro rock.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:24.380
Both the editor and cinematographer are working
to bring us into Chun Li's emotional space,

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:30.210
they want us to share her viewpoint and understand
the contrast in her life, the peace and serenity

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:35.050
of her early years in San Francisco versus
the uncertainty and chaos of Hong Kong.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.800
This is solid fundamental film making.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.790
So what’s the problem?

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:43.230
[Laughs] oh boy is the script terrible.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:49.139
That’s not the only problem, but if we were
to point to a singular core issue that's what

00:05:49.139 --> 00:05:54.610
went wrong with Crouching Pianist Hidden Street
Fighter: the script sucks.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:58.919
The performances leave a lot to be desired,
but it’s hard to give a powerful performance

00:05:58.919 --> 00:06:02.020
when the script consists of gems like this:

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:04.639
my father was an important business man

00:06:04.639 --> 00:06:07.090
Your father was a very well connected business
man.

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:11.160
Kreuk sounds like she's reading a bedtime
story to a three year old.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:16.040
Which, yeah, sure, a great way to really elevate
your video game movie is to condescend to

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:18.320
the audience like they’re actual children,
sure, yeah.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:24.610
With my mother gone, the path in front of
me was empty.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:27.979
I couldn't help feeling like I was being led
somewhere new.

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:33.100
I’m actually willing to bet that this voice
over, like many a terrible narrator past,

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:37.710
was a late addition once they realized the
plot was not particularly compelling on its

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:43.100
own merits and audiences were having trouble
following the setup because it’s boring.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:47.229
However Kreuk’s condescending narration
is not the least of the script's problems.

00:06:47.229 --> 00:06:51.550
For some brevity, let's just make it into
a list of major flaws, though I'm not going

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:53.389
to be able to get to all of these.

00:06:53.389 --> 00:06:57.650
1 - Terrible dialogue
2 - Villain with unclear goals and motivations

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:02.669
3 - Poorly explained stakes
4 - Multiple extraneous characters with dead

00:07:02.669 --> 00:07:06.820
end plotlines
5 - Main character learns nothing worthwhile

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:10.850
and does not change
6 - Changes are made to the source material

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:17.270
that actively makes the story worse
7 - Inconsistent moral core in a movie about

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:18.930
doing the right thing

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:22.740
And that is the bottom line that we’re going
to dig into here: this movie sucks because

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:25.240
the script is terrible.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.270
Let’s talk about that plot, shall we?

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:30.830
As a young child Chun Li wants to be a concert
pianist.

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:32.930
Her family moves to Hong Kong.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:34.900
She is taught Wushu by her father.

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:39.060
However at a young age her father is kidnapped
by Bison, played here by Neal McDonough doing

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:43.330
his best to try and set himself apart from
Raul Julia’s sublime performance of the

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:53.360
character by acting the character with a lilting
Irish brogue.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.590
Chun Li grows up and does become a concert
pianist.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:08:00.919
After a performance one night she receives
an ancient scroll as a gift from an unknown

00:08:00.919 --> 00:08:01.919
person.

00:08:01.919 --> 00:08:06.350
On her way home she sees a man with a spider
web tattoo on his hand get mugged in the subway,

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:10.919
and she calls for help, but no one comes and
then the scene just ends.

00:08:10.919 --> 00:08:14.180
This is the kind of moment that isn’t going
to stand out on the first viewing, because

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:18.990
you mentally assume they’re going to fill
it in down the line, but they don’t.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:25.840
I am, despite multiple viewings, hard pressed
to explain the point or purpose of this mugging

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:26.840
scene.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:28.480
Moving on Chun Li’s mom dies of cancer.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:32.169
Bison has his business partners killed for
no discernible reason.

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:36.610
Bison menaces Chun Li’s father who he has
stashed away in a basement somewhere doing

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:38.510
something.

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:41.990
After her mother’s funeral Chun Li gets
her scroll read and the woman tells her she

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:46.730
needs to find a man named Gen, who’s the
guy with the spider web tattoo, and then I

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:54.319
guess Chun Li becomes homeless in Thailand
because that’s her plan to find Gen?

00:08:54.319 --> 00:08:58.910
This isn't well explained at all, a lot changes
very quickly, and none of her friends or family

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:02.690
seem concerned that she's quitting her job
and moving to Bangkok on a whim shortly following

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:06.829
the death of her mother without making any
prior arrangements for housing, finances,

00:09:06.829 --> 00:09:08.949
or contact.

00:09:08.949 --> 00:09:11.100
We'll come back to this plot point later.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:13.730
Bison acts menacing while talking about real
estate.

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:18.060
Chun Li fights off six guys while starving
and sleep deprived, then collapses after murdering

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:21.850
a man by dropping a shelving unit full of
power tools onto him.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:25.439
She's picked up by Gen who takes her back
to his place and trains her to make fireballs

00:09:25.439 --> 00:09:30.240
in an extended training montage and he tells
Chun Li that Bison is the person who kidnapped

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:35.870
her father, and so Chun Li starts spying on
Bison’s team.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:42.740
Actually, as a part of this he explains that
he used to be part of Shadaloo, Bison's organization,

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:46.850
but he grew a conscience so he started the
Order of the Web to try and repair the damage

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:52.709
that he had done as a criminal, but he’s
only, like, fifty, which would mean these

00:09:52.709 --> 00:09:57.910
ancient scrolls are younger than a New Kids
on the Block poster.

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:02.860
So the next 20 minutes or so are largely concerned
with Bison’s real estate conspiracy.

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:08.749
He is, I guess, extorting the city of Bankok
into selling him the riverside slums so that

00:10:08.749 --> 00:10:13.069
he can kick out all the residents, bulldoze
the neighbourhood, and build a series of luxury

00:10:13.069 --> 00:10:18.470
condos, but I’m not sure why he needs to
do all this extorting because this sounds

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:24.100
like exactly the kind of garbage move cities
are already more than willing to do.

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:28.429
Also there’s a MacGuffin called the White
Rose that Bison is trying to smuggle into

00:10:28.429 --> 00:10:29.730
the city.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:35.470
This sub plot leads to Chun Li following Bison’s
henchwoman Cantana into a nightclub where

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:41.970
we’re going to put the movie on pause and
talk for a second about gaze.

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:45.830
I have talked in the past about the Kuleshov
effect, the principle of Montage, and the

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:50.160
psychological mechanisms through which editing
creates meaning.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:53.489
Now, gaze describes the act of looking.

00:10:53.489 --> 00:10:58.269
In a film context we are principally concerned
with the gaze of the camera, meaning what

00:10:58.269 --> 00:11:02.769
the camera chooses to look at and how it chooses
to look at it, and yes this involves anthropomorphizing

00:11:02.769 --> 00:11:03.769
the movie.

00:11:03.769 --> 00:11:07.739
No better are the ideas of gaze and montage
demonstrated than when a film chooses to use

00:11:07.739 --> 00:11:13.999
its gaze to express the gaze of a character
within the film, which is itself a situation

00:11:13.999 --> 00:11:16.420
manufactured to justify gazing.

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:22.170
So, Chun Li’s plan to get information out
of Cantana is to seduce her, a plan to which

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:28.580
Cantana is potentially receptive because she’s
attracted to women, a detail that is communicated

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:34.910
to us the audience via the camera following
her gaze, and gazing on the women in the dance

00:11:34.910 --> 00:11:35.910
club.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:39.989
This isn’t necessarily condemnation, but
I think it’s useful and instructive to actually

00:11:39.989 --> 00:11:46.179
break down the why and how of this information
exchange, how is it that we, as an audience,

00:11:46.179 --> 00:11:51.619
come to understand that Cantana is attracted
to women purely via the use of juxtaposed

00:11:51.619 --> 00:11:52.819
images.

00:11:52.819 --> 00:11:58.059
Being aware of these kinds of mechanisms makes
us more alert as film viewers, more conscious

00:11:58.059 --> 00:12:03.480
of the ways in which a film is steering our
attention and our emotions, and in turn making

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:08.629
us better equipped to vocalize the messages
that media is presenting us with, in this

00:12:08.629 --> 00:12:14.339
case the way that the film has chosen to justify
staring at women’s asses by using a female

00:12:14.339 --> 00:12:17.760
character as the agent of that staring.

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:22.470
Anyway, this subplot ends with Chun Li fighting
Cantana in the bathroom, and as she’s escaping

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:25.569
from the nightclub she shoots a man in the
chest at point blank range.

00:12:25.569 --> 00:12:28.040
So, yeh, he’s dead.

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:31.319
In a scene that is up there in the realms
of “where did they think they were going

00:12:31.319 --> 00:12:32.319
with this?”

00:12:32.319 --> 00:12:37.679
Bison is using Cantana’s dead body as a
punching bag because she’d already said

00:12:37.679 --> 00:12:38.699
too much.

00:12:38.699 --> 00:12:42.709
Alright, strap in for a second, ‘cus this
is where things really jump off the rails.

00:12:42.709 --> 00:12:48.509
Like, the punching bag scene is definitely
something, Gen fills in Bison’s whole back

00:12:48.509 --> 00:12:49.509
story.

00:12:49.509 --> 00:12:54.309
So, in his youth as the orphan of some Irish
missionaries who died in Thailand he used

00:12:54.309 --> 00:12:59.851
to just steal fish but crossfade time jump
to much later in life when he’s I guess

00:12:59.851 --> 00:13:07.299
no longer homeless he takes his very pregnant
new wife to a cave and kills her by ripping

00:13:07.299 --> 00:13:14.110
their baby out through her belly in order
to put all his goodness into the baby so that

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:19.010
he wouldn't have a conscience and would only
be pure evil.

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:21.170
We'll come back to this one too.

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:23.829
Evil guys attack the secret hide out in the
stupidest way possible.

00:13:23.829 --> 00:13:27.740
They send in ninjas to fight hand to hand
when they have a rocket launcher in the car.

00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:31.240
Both parts of that are stupid, but it does
give us the best line in the film.

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:33.350
I'll do it myself

00:13:33.350 --> 00:13:40.230
In the explosion Gen dies, then Bison hires
Vega to kill Chun Li then Vega promptly gets

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:41.730
his ass kicked.

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:46.059
Now, this next scene, Chun Li physically assaults
a civilian for not giving up private shipping

00:13:46.059 --> 00:13:51.459
manifests and gets some more information about
the White Rose, but for me the scene is more

00:13:51.459 --> 00:13:53.529
instructive because of the sound.

00:13:53.529 --> 00:13:58.459
I haven’t pointed this out up until now,
but the dialogue in the film has overwhelmingly

00:13:58.459 --> 00:14:04.029
been re-dubbed, which is actually part of
why the performances feel so flat and disjointed:

00:14:04.029 --> 00:14:10.299
the emotion of the face and lips just doesn’t
quite match the sound that you’re hearing.

00:14:10.299 --> 00:14:14.049
Also there’s a different character to the
dialogue that’s recorded in a sound booth

00:14:14.049 --> 00:14:16.230
versus the dialogue that’s recorded on set.

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:18.230
It’s subtle, but pervasive.

00:14:18.230 --> 00:14:20.600
Now, if you listen to this guy’s lines,

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:25.009
listen lady, I think it's time for you to
go

00:14:25.009 --> 00:14:28.920
Hear how they’re kinda mumbly and overlapping
with a lot of room noise and echoes?

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:30.790
I think it's time for you to go

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:35.059
I wouldn’t be surprised to find out that
most of the on set sound was like this, which

00:14:35.059 --> 00:14:37.220
is why there’s so much dubbing like this

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:40.249
What does this have to do with my father?

00:14:40.249 --> 00:14:42.779
Many people wanted those connections.

00:14:42.779 --> 00:14:44.559
People like Bison.

00:14:44.559 --> 00:14:46.199
The leader of Shadaloo

00:14:46.199 --> 00:14:48.850
Moving on with the plot, Chun Li gets trapped
and captured.

00:14:48.850 --> 00:14:50.689
Bison kills her dad in front of her.

00:14:50.689 --> 00:14:54.779
She escapes, but takes a grazing shot in a
market, and manages to slip away once a riot

00:14:54.779 --> 00:14:55.779
starts.

00:14:55.779 --> 00:14:59.869
Gen shows up because he’s not dead, just
a dick, uses magic powers to heal her bullet

00:14:59.869 --> 00:15:03.660
wound in seconds, and she finally figures
out how to make a fireball.

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:08.119
Returning to the dock she threatens this guy
again, there's a big fight at the docks, Gen

00:15:08.119 --> 00:15:12.339
fights Balrog, they figure out that the White
Rose is Bison's daughter, Chun Li knocks Bison

00:15:12.339 --> 00:15:16.769
off the roof with a fireball, kills him in
front of his daughter, buries her dad, plants

00:15:16.769 --> 00:15:19.769
some sequel bait, credits roll.

00:15:19.769 --> 00:15:24.239
Did you even notice the two supporting characters
that I cut from the plot?

00:15:24.239 --> 00:15:25.239
These two?

00:15:25.239 --> 00:15:28.060
The interpol agent and the Thai detective?

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:29.519
Nope?

00:15:29.519 --> 00:15:31.269
Guess we didn't need them.

00:15:31.269 --> 00:15:38.540
His name's Bison I’ve tracked him through
eleven major cities on four continents and

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:40.639
never come close, not once.

00:15:40.639 --> 00:15:44.209
They actually take up a substantial chunk
of the screen time, they’re a couple of

00:15:44.209 --> 00:15:50.269
cops in pursuit of Bison and his Shadalaoo
organization, but mostly they just sit slightly

00:15:50.269 --> 00:15:54.490
off to the side of the action or show up after
everything is over.

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:58.299
They’re pretty much just here for padding
and innuendo.

00:15:58.299 --> 00:16:00.549
We should be more aggressive

00:16:00.549 --> 00:16:01.870
Looks like you got that down

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:05.249
Well, unlike you, I don't lose my man

00:16:05.249 --> 00:16:09.809
Alright, [cracking noise] let's start with
the whole "abandoning your past life" storyline.

00:16:09.809 --> 00:16:14.179
The first major flaw in this is that no one
tries to stop her.

00:16:14.179 --> 00:16:18.799
Her mother just died, she's clearly in a deeply
vulnerable place in her life, and not one

00:16:18.799 --> 00:16:23.369
of these people, friends, or extended family
tries to intervene when she quits her job

00:16:23.369 --> 00:16:25.069
and packs up her house.

00:16:25.069 --> 00:16:29.970
this isn't indicative of a plot hole, it's
indicative of a fundamental lack of drama

00:16:29.970 --> 00:16:31.629
or conflict.

00:16:31.629 --> 00:16:37.059
And this all ties in to another issue: she
doesn't actually abandon everything.

00:16:37.059 --> 00:16:40.940
She doesn't get up and walk away from it all,
taking only some bare essentials and living

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:42.629
with the poorest of society.

00:16:42.629 --> 00:16:47.980
Oh, sure, she sleeps in an alley and skips
out on showering, but at the end of the movie

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:51.709
she's moving right back into her multimillion
dollar Hong Kong mansion.

00:16:51.709 --> 00:16:56.759
Living on the streets for a couple weeks was
probably instructive, but she was basically

00:16:56.759 --> 00:16:58.499
a poverty tourist.

00:16:58.499 --> 00:17:03.060
If she ever got tired of living in a slum
there was always a mansion waiting back in

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:04.260
Hong Kong.

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:06.890
She sacrificed nothing.

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:13.870
This ties directly into the character arc:
Chun Li doesn't really learn anything or change.

00:17:13.870 --> 00:17:17.620
At the start of the film she's a confident,
spunky, kindhearted person.

00:17:17.620 --> 00:17:22.530
As the film progresses she dabbles in poverty
tourism, kills three men in cold blood without

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:26.930
remorse, and returns to her life of wealth
as a spunky, kindhearted, slightly more confident

00:17:26.930 --> 00:17:27.930
person.

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:30.890
Now, that's not an irredeemable problem.

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:35.690
Lots of good movies have main characters who
don't learn or change all that much.

00:17:35.690 --> 00:17:41.790
But it IS a problem when your movie opens
with the main character pondering how they

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:45.530
turned into the person they are!

00:17:45.530 --> 00:17:50.230
Sometimes I wonder how I got to be the way
I am.

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:54.000
The writers have explicitly set up a transformation
story.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:58.580
I used to be a little girl who wanted to be
a piano player, now I'm a cold hearted killer

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:00.460
who murders people for the greater good.

00:18:00.460 --> 00:18:05.800
I used to be a selfish, upper class snob,
but I learned the value of hard work, community,

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:06.960
and sacrifice.

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:12.260
These are the kinds of stories that we are,
very literally, being told to expect.

00:18:12.260 --> 00:18:17.480
Any transformation contrast is deeply compromised
by the early scenes of the film, when her

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:18.480
father is kidnapped.

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:23.530
Now, the problem is not the existence of these
scenes, but their proportional length within

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:24.690
the film.

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:28.560
We're shown that she's been exposed to this
criminal world since she was a little girl

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:32.400
and we only spend the briefest time with her
as a civilian.

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:37.320
This is a really good example of how movies
need to function emotionally and not just

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:38.320
literally.

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:42.870
We can look at the opening of the film and
extrapolate that logically everything between

00:18:42.870 --> 00:18:48.170
her father being kidnapped and her success
as an adult was more or less normal but we

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:51.730
don’t feel the respective weight of these
things

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:57.150
Because it's such a tiny part of the opening
her time as a concert pianist feels more like

00:18:57.150 --> 00:19:02.310
a brief interlude in a life of violence, a
hobby that she took up to pass the time waiting

00:19:02.310 --> 00:19:08.110
for revenge, rather than the normal world
that she's pulled out of, and what little

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:12.590
we see of her in that stage of her life is
basically the same person we see at the end

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:13.590
of the film.

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:20.750
The only thing that changes is the number
of living parents and felony murders.

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:26.100
Next up is the whole thing where Bison transfers
his goodness into a little baby.

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:28.340
I have a couple problems with this scene.

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:33.510
First, and least important, is that the intensity
and violence of the scene is considerably

00:19:33.510 --> 00:19:37.660
out of place given the tone and treatment
of violence throughout the rest of the film.

00:19:37.660 --> 00:19:42.310
It's an extremely jarring kick-the-dog scene
that's meant to show the audience just how

00:19:42.310 --> 00:19:48.630
evil the bad guy is, but I think it becomes
less effective because it's so relatively

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:49.630
extreme.

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:53.850
It's such a sharp break from almost everything
else that it pulls you out to a place where

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:58.500
it stops being the actions of a character
and becomes the decision of some filmmakers.

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:02.670
You’re not thinking about the moment and
its place in the emotional tapestry of the

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:08.510
movie, you’re thinking about why the people
who made this made it the way they did.

00:20:08.510 --> 00:20:12.520
The only other scene that comes close is where
Bison is using Cantana’s body as a punching

00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:17.620
bag, while everything else is sanitized, bloodless,
fantasy violence.

00:20:17.620 --> 00:20:22.280
Beyond that is the implications of this scene
on the metaphysical world of the film.

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:27.270
So, first, things that happen in movies don’t
need to be true.

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:33.820
Like, characters can believe something without
that thing being diegetically factual.

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:39.280
It's one thing for this myth to exist, for
Gen to tell Chun Li this mystical rumour that

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:45.670
he heard, but it's effectively confirmed later
in the film that his daughter is, in fact,

00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:49.050
the vessel holding all the goodness of his
soul.

00:20:49.050 --> 00:20:53.860
That means that in the world of Street Fighter
good and bad are tangible products that can

00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:57.180
be transferred from one person to another.

00:20:57.180 --> 00:21:02.240
While the implications on free will and morality
are staggering, most importantly it does nothing

00:21:02.240 --> 00:21:05.470
to actually improve the story at all.

00:21:05.470 --> 00:21:08.460
It makes Bison less comprehensible.

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:10.590
It doesn’t make his goals any clearer.

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:15.810
We still have no idea what he's up to, what
he's doing on a day to day basis, and what

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:17.710
his end goal is.

00:21:17.710 --> 00:21:19.440
It actually makes it worse.

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:24.270
By offloading the explanation onto supernatural
evil the screenwriters fall into the trap

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:30.060
where the villain’s actions are tautologically
justified by them being the villain.

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:33.730
It’s fine for melodrama, Raul Julia understood
that

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:42.920
The day Bison graced your village was the
most important day of your life.

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:43.940
But for me?

00:21:43.940 --> 00:21:46.620
It was Tuesday.

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:51.050
But Legend of Chun Li is trying to take itself
seriously.

00:21:51.050 --> 00:22:00.970
There's one thing I learned in the slums:
when people are hungry there's nothing they

00:22:00.970 --> 00:22:08.990
won't do.

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:11.330
Everyone has a price.

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:15.600
There's a plot in here about buying up waterfront
property for redevelopment, but they're extorting

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:20.110
the city board into making the sale which
makes no sense.

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:25.530
Was the board zoning the area as a slum for
purely altruistic reasons?

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:29.830
It's like the writers couldn't decide if Bison
should be using black magic, private military,

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:36.060
shady business practices, or a Chinatown style
shakedown, so they tried to squeeze them all

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:37.060
in.

00:22:37.060 --> 00:22:40.890
It's hard to care about any of them since
the stakes are so vague.

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:45.140
The people who live in the slum get kicked
out, then vanish never to be seen or mentioned

00:22:45.140 --> 00:22:48.580
again, not that we ever really connected with
them in the first place.

00:22:48.580 --> 00:22:53.780
The big showdown at the end with Interpol,
Bison, and Chun Li doesn't even have anything

00:22:53.780 --> 00:22:59.140
to do with the whole slum buyout anyway, and
the plot line is never resolved.

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:03.260
The only reason anyone cares about the White
Rose shipment is because they think it might

00:23:03.260 --> 00:23:08.900
be a weapon, even though Bison’s whole plan
is pretty much just capitalism.

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:13.460
To top it all off, the changes to the source
material actively make the story worse.

00:23:13.460 --> 00:23:19.310
In her original incarnations Chun Li is alternately
an undercover cop or an Interpol agent.

00:23:19.310 --> 00:23:23.590
Changing her from a cop to a concert pianist
does nothing to raise the stakes or make the

00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:24.610
character more relatable.

00:23:24.610 --> 00:23:29.680
In fact it makes the story hard to tell because
you now have to justify all kinds of crazy

00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:34.140
crap that's super easy to hand wave if she
works for interpol.

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:35.720
Why is she in Thailand?

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:37.670
Why does she know martial arts?

00:23:37.670 --> 00:23:41.720
Why does she have combat training and not
freak out the first time someone pulls a gun

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:42.720
on her?

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:46.190
Why does she care about the legal front of
a criminal organization?

00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:53.630
all of this action nonsense is just explained
in one line if she works for Interpol.

00:23:53.630 --> 00:23:57.810
The character conflict comes baked in: when
she confronts Bison does she stick with her

00:23:57.810 --> 00:24:03.550
training and let the courts bring him to justice,
or does she extract revenge and kill him herself?

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:08.570
When she meets Gen does she stick with Interpol
with its superior resources, but strict rules

00:24:08.570 --> 00:24:13.400
and expectations, or become a vigilante and
join the Order of the Web?

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:17.970
Instead we're asked to believe that a concert
pianist who learned kung fu as a kid is going

00:24:17.970 --> 00:24:23.370
to be willing to throw herself at a well armed
international criminal organization and not

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:25.570
wind up as Jane Doe floating down the river.

00:24:25.570 --> 00:24:30.960
So, they wanted to legitimize the video game
adaptation and they screwed it up with an

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:32.470
awful script.

00:24:32.470 --> 00:24:35.340
Every single problem with this movie comes
back to the script.

00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:39.010
The characters frequently make decisions based
on information they don't have because the

00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:43.830
script needs to get them somewhere, the villains
are an indistinct mass made of every bad guy

00:24:43.830 --> 00:24:48.750
trope possible, resulting in an antagonist
that's too confusing to take seriously, and

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:52.210
entire chunks of the film are flat out useless
to the story.

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:56.770
the funny thing is that they failed so hard
that even when talking about video game movies,

00:24:56.770 --> 00:25:01.980
a sub-genre that is peppered with examples
that plumb the depths of shoddy, hilarious,

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:08.010
and hilariously shoddy filmmaking, that they
made something most notable for how forgettable

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:09.010
it is.

00:25:09.010 --> 00:25:10.010
It does all make the movie somewhat unique.

00:25:10.010 --> 00:25:13.640
You're not a schoolgirl anymore.

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:17.940
Really in all the agonizing over when video
game movies are going to finally be good we

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:22.500
miss that they already peaked with Super Mario
Bros. in 1993.

00:25:22.500 --> 00:25:30.400
Okay, look, how many Marios are there between
the two of ya?

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:31.400
There's three: there's Mario Mario and Luigi
Mario

00:25:31.400 --> 00:25:32.400
Mike.

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:33.400
Mike!

00:25:33.400 --> 00:25:33.406
Help these Marios around the side.

